<p>A surprising number of flowers are named for people:</p>
<ul>
<li>begonia: after Michel Bégon, a former governor of Saint-Domingue (now Haiti)</li>
<li>camellia: after Jesuit botanist Georg Joseph Kamel</li>
<li>dahlia: after Anders Dahl, a student of Carl Linnaeus</li>
<li>forsythia: after Scottish botanist William Forsyth</li>
<li>fuchsia: after German botanist Leonhart Fuchs</li>
<li>gardenia: after Scottish naturalist Alexander Garden</li>
<li>magnolia: after French botanist Pierre Magnol</li>
<li>poinsettia: after Joel Roberts Poinsett, the first U.S. minister to Mexico, who is credited with bringing the flower to the United States</li>
<li>wisteria: after American physician Caspar Wistar</li>
<li>zinnia: after German botanist Johann Gottfried Zinn</li>
</ul>
<p>The ackee fruit of Jamaica is named <em>Blighia sapida</em> after William Bligh, who discovered it in his travels after the mutiny on HMS <em>Bounty</em>.</p>
<p>(Via Maxwell W. Nurnberg, <a href="https://archive.org/details/wondersinwords0000maxw/page/20/mode/2up"><em>Wonders in Words</em></a>, 1968.)</p>
